#
# prima-materia - the searches of the run on Long, The Secret Science Behind
# Miracles, 2026-10-19
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:HunaFailureConditionSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Where the Huna work puts a failure, and whether it says how it chose its cases"@en, "Wohin das Huna-Werk einen Fehlschlag legt, und ob es sagt, wie es seine Fälle wählte"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), in the archive.org full text: chapters II, III, IV, X, XI, XV, XVIII, XIX, XXI and XXII read through, and the whole text searched by term."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A condition named in advance under which a practice of the work is to be known to have failed; a report of a practice that did fail; and any statement of how the reported cases were selected from those that came out otherwise."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read through in the chapters named. Term search over the whole text: test (30 hits), failed or failure (39), trick (16), fraud (2), sceptic or skeptic (2), did not come true (1); every hit in the chapters read was read in place, the hits in chapters not read were read at two hundred characters either side."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HunaOmitsTheDreamsThatFailed, pmc:HunaHeardOfNoFailure, pmc:HunaNoResultsMeansAComplex, pmc:HunaThePictureBlocksTheContact, pmc:HunaGrumblingStopsTheDreams, pmc:HunaGlanceAsideBlisters, pmc:HunaTheBurnIsReadAsGuilt, pmc:HunaKahunaSuccessAverage, pmc:HunaEightBooksForetold ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-19" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-19" ;
    skos:note "Found, and in two shapes that the work keeps apart without saying so. Failures it reports are put inside a person, almost always as a complex of guilt or doubt in the low self of the operator or the patient: the dreams stop because the low self took a dislike to the practice, the contact fails over a photograph, the death prayer takes only the guilty, healing is withheld until the complex is cleared, and an absence of any result is itself the sign of a complex. Failures it does not report are kept out by statement: the dreams that did not come true are omitted, and no failure of the wind prayer reached the author. One prediction in the chapters read was printed before it fell due and carries a fixed count, the eight books of chapter XIX."@en ;
    skos:note "Seen in the term search in chapters not read and not entered, so that the next reader of the work starts there: in chapter VIII a test the author made of reading the thoughts of students from behind in a study hall; in chapter XVII a sentence that some candidates of the Burmese fire walk failed; in chapters XII and XIII sittings of the medium Mirabelli quoted from the séance literature. None of them was read at the wording beyond the hit."@en .

pmp:HunaAscriptionCounterSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Hawaiian sources know unihipili and aumakua as selves of the living"@en, "Ob die hawaiischen Quellen unihipili und aumakua als Selbste der Lebenden kennen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Lorrin Andrews, A Dictionary of the Hawaiian Language (Honolulu: Henry M. Whitney, 1865), archive.org full text under dictionaryofhawa00andrrich, 2,353,593 bytes; David Malo, Hawaiian Antiquities (Moolelo Hawaii), translated by N. B. Emerson (Honolulu: Hawaiian Gazette Co., 1903), archive.org full text under hawaiianantiquit00malouoft, 917,896 bytes."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The counter-search for pmc:HunaUnihipiliIsTheLowSelf and pmc:HunaAumakuaIsTheHighSelf: a Hawaiian or nineteenth-century source that uses unihipili, uhane or aumakua for a part of a living person, or one that says what else they name."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In Andrews: unihipili (4 hits), the hyphenated headword forms U-ni-hi-pi-li and U-ha-ne, aumakua (7), A-KU-A-AU-MA-KU-A (1), every hit read in place with the page from the running heads. In Malo: unihipili (3), aumakua (18), uhane (4), akua noho in both spellings; chapter XXXII and its notes read through, the other hits read in place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:WhetherUnihipiliNamesASelfOfTheLiving, pmp:HawaiianHoonohonohoAkua, pmc:HawaiianUnihipiliKeeperDiesOfNeglect ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-19" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-19" ;
    skos:note "Opposing in both sources and on the point at issue. Andrews gives unihipili as the leg and arm bones bound up together and worshipped, and as one name of the class of gods called akuanoho with aumakua, the departed spirits of deceased persons (p. 124), and aumakua as a class of ancient trustworthy gods, the deified ancestors (pp. 33, 44); Malo makes both terms names for a spirit that comes to dwell with a dead man's bones set up with an idol (chapter XXXII, section 2), and Emerson's note distinguishes them as an ancestral deity and an artificial one made by a priest (pp. 157-158). Neither source uses either word for a part of a living person. Andrews does record the belief the work starts from, that men had two souls, under uhane; the work quotes that note and agrees with it, and it is not the point in dispute."@en ;
    skos:note "Not searched: Kamakau's newspaper series and its translation of 1964, the Pukui-Elbert dictionary, Pukui, Haertig and Lee, Nana i ke Kumu, which treats aumakua at length, and the modern scholarly critiques of Huna that the registration refers to; all are copyright works that were not looked for in this run. The finding therefore rests on two nineteenth-century sources, both written in or for the mission, and says so at the dispute."@en .

pmp:HunaLayTestSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Hawaiian sources record a test that laymen put to a priest's claim"@en, "Ob die hawaiischen Quellen eine Probe verzeichnen, die Laien an den Anspruch eines Priesters legten"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "David Malo, Hawaiian Antiquities (Moolelo Hawaii), translated by N. B. Emerson (Honolulu: Hawaiian Gazette Co., 1903), chapter XXXII read through; Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), by the term search named at pmp:HunaFailureConditionSearch."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Beifang of the counter-search: a test, put by people who doubted it, to a claim of a Hawaiian practitioner, with a criterion fixed before the answer; and whether the modern work that claims to report the Hawaiian secret records any such test."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In Malo, chapter XXXII read through; in Long, test, sceptic or skeptic, fraud and trick over the whole text, every hit read at least at two hundred characters either side."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HawaiianAkuaNohoTestedByTheBundle, pmc:HawaiianAkuaNohoHeldFraudByMany ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-19" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-19" ;
    skos:note "Found in Malo: two tests by doubters, the gesture under the robe and the wrapped bundle, each scored both ways, with a statement that perhaps a majority held the possessing gods frauds. Not found in Long: none of his hits for the terms is a test put by Hawaiians to a priest; his tests are his own or those of white visitors and psychical researchers, and the one Polynesian ordeal he reports, a Tahitian fire walk, reads the outcome as guilt and not as a test of the protection."@en .
